//? Title: Newton–Raphson root finding
//? Subtitle: Actual tangents, callback schedules and local convergence controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Find a scalar root by following tangent-line zeros from a supplied starting
point. Actual canonical callback and step records explain the original
square-root-of-two experiment without retracing its updates. A repeated root and
a bounded two-cycle distinguish local quadratic behavior from a global
convergence guarantee, while explicit callback counts clarify the legacy
iteration field.
)

\toc

> The tangent update and its assumptions

Linearizing f at x gives f(x)+f′(x)(t−x). Its zero is x_next=x−f(x)/f′(x),
provided the requested derivative is nonzero. Near a simple root r with
sufficient smoothness and f′(r)≠0, Taylor expansion yields
e_next≈f″(r)e²/(2f′(r)). This is local quadratic convergence under a suitable
starting point, until floating rounding dominates. It is not a guarantee from an
arbitrary seed. A root of multiplicity m instead gives leading error factor1−1/m
for ordinary Newton, so convergence is typically linear without a multiplicity
correction.

The stopping test is the strict absolute residual abs(f(x))<tol. A small
residual does not by itself bound distance to a root without additional
assumptions such as a suitable derivative lower bound. The supplied derivative
must describe the function; the API cannot validate that mathematical relation
from isolated callback values.

> One hand-checkable tangent

For f(x)=x²−2 at x=2, the actual f value is2 and derivative4, giving the actual
next point1.5. Every number in the diagram reads the first canonical step
record. The native trace records f and derivative calls in their original
sequential order; no report-side Newton loop is used.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/newton_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/newton_raphson.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto s = dm::algorithms::newton_raphson_square_study();
    auto a = s.trace.steps.front();
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("point", {0, 0}, std::format("Actual x={}\nf(x)={}", a.x, a.fx));
    d.node("slope", {6, 0},
           std::format("Actual derivative {}\ncorrection {}", a.derivative,
                       a.correction));
    d.node("next", {12, 0},
           std::format("Actual tangent zero\nx_next={}", a.next));
    d.edge("point", "slope").edge("slope", "next");
    std::print("First actual update: {} - {} / {} = {}.\n", a.x, a.fx,
               a.derivative, a.next);
    d.save_svg("build/newton_tiny.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
First actual update: 2 - 2 / 4 = 1.5.
\image(figures/newton_tiny.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual first tangent step of the original x²−2 experiment.)
\alttext(The inspected point2, function value2 and derivative4 produce the recorded next point1.5.)

> Retain the original sqrt(2) run and tangent figure

The original controls are x0=2, default tolerance10⁻¹⁰ and update cap100. The
native study returns its actual inspected points, f values and tangent
crossings, together with a fixed display grid for x²−2. At default tolerance it
performs four assignments and five residual inspections. The final residual is
small enough for that tolerance, but the original assertion that four updates
already give full binary64 precision is too strong. The program prints the
actual error against native sqrt(2) rather than assuming a number of correct
digits.

The legacy RootResult.iterations field counts one-based loop inspections, so it
reports five for this run, including the accepted final inspection. It is
distinct from the new actual updates counter. An initially accepted root reports
one legacy iteration and zero updates when the budget is positive. Exhausting
the budget performs a final f callback at the last assigned point, including a
zero-budget call at x0.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/newton_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/newton_raphson.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto s = dm::algorithms::newton_raphson_square_study();
    std::print("| actual inspected state | actual x | actual f(x) | reference x error |\n| ---: | ---: | " "---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < s.states.size(); ++i)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", i, s.states[i], s.values[i],
                   s.reference_errors[i]);
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.lines(s.curve_x, s.curve_y, "x squared minus 2", {100, 116, 139}).segments(s.tangent_x, s.tangent_y, s.tangent_next, std::vector<double>(s.tangent_next.size(), 0), {192, 59, 50}, 2).points(s.states, s.values, "actual inspections", {36, 105, 172}, 6).abline_h(0, {148, 163, 184}, 1).abline_v(s.reference, {22, 163, 74}, 1).title("Original Newton run: actual tangent steps from x0=2").x_label("x").y_label("actual f(x)").save_svg("build/newton_original.svg");
    auto r = s.trace.run;
    std::print("\nLegacy iterations {}; actual updates {}; f calls {}; derivative calls {}; actual stop " "{}.\n",
               r.result.iterations, r.updates, r.function_calls,
               r.derivative_calls,
               dm::algorithms::newton_raphson_stop_name(r.stop));
}
