> Flat derivatives, a two-cycle and zero-budget controls

For x³−2x+2 starting at0, Newton alternates exactly between0 and1 in this
arithmetic. A cap of eight updates ends without residual convergence. For x²−2
starting at0, the nonzero residual and exactly zero derivative stop before
division. Neither failure proves that a root does not exist. Initial-root and
zero-budget cases demonstrate the actual callback schedule independently of any
asymptotic rate claim.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/newton_controls.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/newton_raphson.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    using F = std::function<double(double)>;
    struct Case {
        std::string name;
        F f, df;
        double seed;
        int cap;
    };
    std::vector<Case> cases{{"two-cycle", [](double x) {
        return x * x * x - 2 * x + 2;
    }, [](double x) {
        return 3 * x * x - 2;
    }, 0, 8}, {"flat", [](double x) {
        return x * x - 2;
    }, [](double x) {
        return 2 * x;
    }, 0, 100}, {"initial root", [](double x) {
        return x - 1;
    }, [](double) {
        return 1.;
    }, 1, 100}, {"zero budget", [](double x) {
        return x * x - 2;
    }, [](double x) { return 2 * x; }, 2, 0}};
    std::vector<std::string> labels;
    std::vector<double> updates;
    std::print("| control | actual stop | converged | actual f calls | actual derivative calls | actual " "updates |\n| --- | --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (auto &c : cases) {
        auto r =
            dm::algorithms::newton_raphson_run(c.f, c.df, c.seed, 1e-10, c.cap);
        labels.push_back(c.name);
        updates.push_back(r.updates);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", c.name,
                   dm::algorithms::newton_raphson_stop_name(r.stop),
                   r.result.converged, r.function_calls, r.derivative_calls,
                   r.updates);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(updates, labels, "actual updates");
    p.title("Newton controls: numerical stops and actual assignments").x_label(
        "fixed control").y_label("actual tangent updates").save_svg(
        "build/newton_controls.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| control | actual stop | converged | actual f calls | actual derivative calls | actual updates |
| --- | --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| two-cycle | iteration-limit | false | 9 | 8 | 8 |
| flat | zero-derivative | false | 1 | 1 | 0 |
| initial root | residual | true | 1 | 0 | 0 |
| zero budget | iteration-limit | false | 1 | 0 | 0 |
\image(figures/newton_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual update counts for a two-cycle, flat derivative, initial root and zero budget.)
\alttext(The computed table distinguishes numerical failure from a claim about root existence.)

> Complete guards, cost and references

Both callbacks, finite starting point and finite nonnegative tolerance are
validated before any callback. The update cap must be nonnegative and below
INT_MAX. Tolerance zero disables the strict residual acceptance test. A
nonfinite f or derivative return throws a domain error; a nonfinite quotient or
assigned point throws an overflow error. Callback exceptions propagate
unchanged. Small nonzero derivatives are not silently thresholded, and there is
no hidden damping or bracket.

The ordinary producer uses O(I) work plus actual callback costs and O(1) driver
storage. The owned trace retains three input controls plus one fixed record per
actual callback and inspection, exactly3+calls+inspections logical units. The
default1000000-unit quota is admitted online before the corresponding
record/callback, zero disables; later failure returns no partial trace but
cannot undo earlier callback effects. This is not a byte or peak-memory claim.

[NIST DLMF, §3.8, Nonlinear Equations|https://dlmf.nist.gov/3.8] provides the
numerical-method context and local convergence distinctions. The actual tangent
geometry, repeated-root control and bounded cycle make the implemented stopping
behavior observable without treating a residual threshold or a fixed iteration
count as a global accuracy certificate.

